    "dishonor": {
      "word": "DISHONOR",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Lack of honor; disgrace; ignominy; shame; reproach. It was not meet for us to see the king's dishonor. Ezra iv. 14. His honor rooted in dishonor stood. Tennyson.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Law)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The nonpayment or nonacceptance of commercial paper by the party on whom it is drawn.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To deprive of honor; to disgrace; to bring reproach or shame on; to treat with indignity, or as unworthy in the sight of others; to stain the character of; to lessen the reputation of; as, the duelist dishonors himself to maintain his honor. Nothing . . . that may dishonor Our law, or stain my vow of Nazarite. Milton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To violate the chastity of; to debauch. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To refuse or decline to accept or pay; -- said of a bill, check, note, or draft which is due or presented; as, to dishonor a bill exchange. debauch; pollute.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
